1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,371
Here is a training run
that finished without a single error.

2
00:00:03,691 --> 00:00:07,467
The job succeeded,
and the run exists in Weights & Biases.

3
00:00:07,787 --> 00:00:10,709
Its config is there,
and so are its system charts.

4
00:00:11,683 --> 00:00:14,798
But there is no loss chart,
and no accuracy chart.

5
00:00:15,118 --> 00:00:16,291
Where are the curves?

6
00:00:16,611 --> 00:00:18,830
Here is the line that trained this model.

7
00:00:19,150 --> 00:00:22,073
Hold that thought;
we come back to it in a few minutes.

8
00:00:22,317 --> 00:00:26,776
The gray tag in the corner names
the lecture slides behind each idea.

9
00:00:27,096 --> 00:00:30,937
On the lecture's roadmap,
experiment tracking is set up early,

10
00:00:30,937 --> 00:00:34,839
together with the baseline models,
right after data collection.

11
00:00:35,083 --> 00:00:38,176
This is the exam edition of Lecture 13.

12
00:00:38,496 --> 00:00:42,870
It follows five places
where a training workflow quietly breaks:

13
00:00:42,870 --> 00:00:46,267
the run record,
the package, the GPUs, the batch,

14
00:00:46,267 --> 00:00:47,585
and the job itself.

15
00:00:47,833 --> 00:00:51,972
Each part ends with one rule:
if the question says this, think that.

16
00:00:52,292 --> 00:00:55,662
Three times,
a quiz-style question appears on screen.

17
00:00:55,982 --> 00:00:57,299
Pause, pick an answer,

18
00:00:57,299 --> 00:00:59,993
and then hear why
each wrong option is wrong.

19
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:03,034
The lecture names the reason
to track at all.

20
00:01:03,354 --> 00:01:06,328
If the question says
experiment tracking, think:

21
00:01:06,328 --> 00:01:09,241
what produced this result,
and can I repeat it?

22
00:01:11,583 --> 00:01:13,247
What should a run record hold?

23
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:17,137
The lecture lists seven things:
the environment, the code,

24
00:01:17,137 --> 00:01:21,076
the data, the model, the metrics,
the artifacts, and your notes.

25
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:23,470
Most of these already have a home.

26
00:01:23,790 --> 00:01:27,107
Code lives in GitHub,
data in a versioning system,

27
00:01:27,107 --> 00:01:31,086
and the environment in the pyproject file
and the lock file.

28
00:01:31,406 --> 00:01:34,222
Weights, metrics and logs
get a question mark.

29
00:01:34,466 --> 00:01:35,789
One word on data.

30
00:01:36,109 --> 00:01:40,043
A data version is a pair:
the archived snapshot in a bucket,

31
00:01:40,043 --> 00:01:44,173
and the version record that names it,
linked by a content hash.

32
00:01:44,493 --> 00:01:48,004
This trainer instead downloads
a fixed release of the data,

33
00:01:48,004 --> 00:01:50,146
and never writes it into the config.

34
00:01:50,383 --> 00:01:52,529
Recording by hand gets forgotten,

35
00:01:52,529 --> 00:01:55,780
so the lecture automates it
with Weights & Biases.

36
00:01:56,100 --> 00:01:59,642
Picture a ledger
in which every training run is one row.

37
00:01:59,883 --> 00:02:04,427
The repo trainer,
task.py, talks to that ledger in four calls.

38
00:02:04,747 --> 00:02:09,078
First it logs in with a key passed
as a flag, never written in the code.

39
00:02:09,316 --> 00:02:14,381
Then wandb.init opens a new row,
with a project, a name, and a config:

40
00:02:14,381 --> 00:02:17,999
learning rate, epochs, batch size,
and model name.

41
00:02:18,319 --> 00:02:21,000
It also makes that row the active run,

42
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,527
and the pointer called
wandb.run now points at it.

43
00:02:24,766 --> 00:02:28,380
After training,
the script adds one more config value,

44
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:29,651
the execution time.

45
00:02:29,971 --> 00:02:35,156
Then wandb.run.finish() seals the row,
and the pointer falls back to nothing.

46
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:38,331
Notice what this run recorded on its own:

47
00:02:38,331 --> 00:02:42,120
the config,
and system charts such as CPU and memory.

48
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,083
It did not record a loss.

49
00:02:44,403 --> 00:02:46,642
Something still has to log the metrics.

50
00:02:47,483 --> 00:02:50,325
If the question says record of a run, think:

51
00:02:50,325 --> 00:02:54,523
init opens it, a logger writes the metrics,
and finish closes it.

52
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:55,918
First question.

53
00:02:56,238 --> 00:02:59,118
The run shows its config but no loss curve.

54
00:02:59,438 --> 00:03:01,465
Which change puts a loss curve on it?

55
00:03:01,785 --> 00:03:05,198
Three options can be ruled out
with what you know already.

56
00:03:05,518 --> 00:03:06,884
Pause here if you want.

57
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:19,162
The answer is C:

58
00:03:19,162 --> 00:03:24,241
pass the WandbMetricsLogger
in the callbacks of fit, created after init.

59
00:03:24,561 --> 00:03:26,822
A: a name only labels the row.

60
00:03:27,142 --> 00:03:31,132
B: one number in the config
is not a history over epochs.

61
00:03:31,452 --> 00:03:33,926
D: logging in again writes no metrics.

62
00:03:34,166 --> 00:03:37,238
A callback is a pen
that Keras holds during training.

63
00:03:37,558 --> 00:03:40,884
At the end of every epoch it calls
wandb.log,

64
00:03:40,884 --> 00:03:43,396
and that call writes to wandb.run:

65
00:03:43,396 --> 00:03:48,716
whichever row is active right now,
not the run the pen was created with.

66
00:03:48,950 --> 00:03:51,318
The lecture's notebook does exactly this.

67
00:03:51,638 --> 00:03:53,354
It calls init, and then fit,

68
00:03:53,354 --> 00:03:56,480
with a callbacks list
that holds the WandbCallback.

69
00:03:56,716 --> 00:03:59,788
By default,
that is one history entry per epoch.

70
00:04:00,108 --> 00:04:02,263
Per batch happens only if you ask.

71
00:04:02,816 --> 00:04:04,288
Now four experiments.

72
00:04:04,608 --> 00:04:05,845
First, no pen.

73
00:04:06,165 --> 00:04:09,259
Fit runs two epochs,
and the strip stays empty.

74
00:04:09,579 --> 00:04:11,158
That is our opening run.

75
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,495
Second: init, fit for two epochs,

76
00:04:13,495 --> 00:04:16,669
then fit for four more,
with no finish in between.

77
00:04:16,989 --> 00:04:21,021
The pointer never moved,
so ticks three to six land on the same row.

78
00:04:21,341 --> 00:04:23,005
One run, six entries.

79
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:26,480
Third: finish after the first fit,
then init again,

80
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:29,202
and fit four epochs with the same callback.

81
00:04:29,522 --> 00:04:31,762
The first row is sealed with two entries.

82
00:04:32,082 --> 00:04:36,229
The callback was made during row one,
but it follows the pointer,

83
00:04:36,229 --> 00:04:38,780
so the new row gets entries one to four.

84
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:42,195
Fourth:
finish, then fit again without a new init.

85
00:04:42,515 --> 00:04:43,929
The first epoch trains,

86
00:04:43,929 --> 00:04:46,819
and then the pen follows the pointer
to nothing

87
00:04:46,819 --> 00:04:50,152
and stops with an error:
you must call init before log.

88
00:04:50,472 --> 00:04:52,008
Nothing reaches any row.

89
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:55,663
Creating the logger
with no active run fails at once.

90
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:56,903
Now the hook.

91
00:04:57,223 --> 00:05:01,625
The repo trainer imports both callbacks
but passes none to fit,

92
00:05:01,625 --> 00:05:05,607
so its run has a config and system charts,
and no curves.

93
00:05:05,927 --> 00:05:09,852
The lecture's middle line,
the callback, is the line that is missing.

94
00:05:10,916 --> 00:05:15,673
One detail: the notebook warns
that the WandbCallback is deprecated.

95
00:05:15,993 --> 00:05:20,047
For the curves,
its replacement is the WandbMetricsLogger.

96
00:05:20,283 --> 00:05:23,246
If the question
counts history entries, think:

97
00:05:23,246 --> 00:05:27,238
one per epoch, into whichever run is open
when the epoch ends.

98
00:05:27,558 --> 00:05:30,907
A finish ends the count,
and a new init starts a new one.

99
00:05:32,050 --> 00:05:35,899
The lecture moves training
to the cloud in three steps:

100
00:05:35,899 --> 00:05:37,929
a Python file with arguments,

101
00:05:37,929 --> 00:05:41,778
a package in a bucket,
and a training job on Vertex AI.

102
00:05:42,017 --> 00:05:44,463
Here is the package folder: setup.py,

103
00:05:44,463 --> 00:05:49,355
and a trainer folder with an empty init file
and the two training scripts.

104
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,627
setup.py keeps two lists.

105
00:05:51,947 --> 00:05:57,824
install_requires is a shopping list that pip
downloads from the Python Package Index;

106
00:05:57,824 --> 00:05:59,968
here, one pinned wandb version.

107
00:06:00,288 --> 00:06:04,527
find_packages() decides
which of your own folders get installed:

108
00:06:04,527 --> 00:06:06,581
only folders with an init file.

109
00:06:06,817 --> 00:06:09,517
The packaging script
tars the package folder,

110
00:06:09,517 --> 00:06:12,278
compresses it,
and copies it into your bucket.

111
00:06:12,598 --> 00:06:18,422
The job script points Vertex AI at that file
and names the module: trainer.task.

112
00:06:18,667 --> 00:06:20,032
Here is the key fact.

113
00:06:20,352 --> 00:06:23,911
Your own Docker image
packages, uploads and submits,

114
00:06:23,911 --> 00:06:25,280
but it never trains.

115
00:06:25,600 --> 00:06:28,525
The job runs
in Google's prebuilt training image,

116
00:06:28,525 --> 00:06:31,509
and your code reaches it only
through the tarball.

117
00:06:31,750 --> 00:06:36,102
That image already ships
TensorFlow, scikit-learn and requests,

118
00:06:36,102 --> 00:06:40,454
so the trainer imports them
although setup.py never names them.

119
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:43,431
On the job machine, pip unwraps the tarball.

120
00:06:43,751 --> 00:06:47,953
The shopping list fetches wandb,
and the trainer package is installed.

121
00:06:48,273 --> 00:06:51,516
Then Vertex runs python -m trainer.task.

122
00:06:51,750 --> 00:06:54,641
Now add a teammate's module, augment.py,

123
00:06:54,641 --> 00:06:57,894
a made-up example,
beside the package folder.

124
00:06:58,214 --> 00:07:02,630
The script tars only that folder,
so the module never leaves your laptop.

125
00:07:02,950 --> 00:07:08,326
Placed next to setup.py instead,
it travels, but find_packages() skips it.

126
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:11,265
The task reaches import augment;

127
00:07:11,265 --> 00:07:14,699
Python searches
its import path and finds nothing.

128
00:07:15,019 --> 00:07:20,374
ModuleNotFoundError, right under the line
that says every dependency installed.

129
00:07:20,694 --> 00:07:21,764
Locally it worked,

130
00:07:21,764 --> 00:07:24,619
because the file sat
in the folder you ran from.

131
00:07:24,867 --> 00:07:28,299
The fix:
put the file inside the trainer folder,

132
00:07:28,299 --> 00:07:29,944
or list it in setup.py.

133
00:07:30,264 --> 00:07:34,296
A new import line alone moves nothing;
the file has to travel.

134
00:07:34,533 --> 00:07:36,944
Adding it to install_requires is wrong.

135
00:07:37,264 --> 00:07:40,226
That list downloads
from the Python Package Index,

136
00:07:40,226 --> 00:07:41,664
never from your tarball,

137
00:07:41,664 --> 00:07:45,499
and a package there with that name
would be someone else's code.

138
00:07:45,733 --> 00:07:51,105
If the question says dependencies installed
but ModuleNotFoundError, think:

139
00:07:51,105 --> 00:07:55,760
the file never travelled inside the package
that setup.py builds.

140
00:07:57,000 --> 00:07:59,901
The lecture sorts training hardware
into three shapes.

141
00:08:00,221 --> 00:08:04,253
One machine with one GPU
uses OneDeviceStrategy.

142
00:08:04,573 --> 00:08:08,477
One machine with several GPUs
uses MirroredStrategy.

143
00:08:08,797 --> 00:08:12,253
Several machines use
MultiWorkerMirroredStrategy.

144
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:14,463
The repo sits on the middle row.

145
00:08:14,783 --> 00:08:21,860
Its multi GPU script asks Vertex AI
for one machine, type n1-standard-32,

146
00:08:21,860 --> 00:08:23,508
with two T4 GPUs.

147
00:08:23,828 --> 00:08:26,729
A replica count of one there
means one machine.

148
00:08:26,967 --> 00:08:32,650
The trainer creates the MirroredStrategy
and asks how many replicas are in sync:

149
00:08:32,650 --> 00:08:34,071
one per GPU, so two.

150
00:08:34,391 --> 00:08:38,965
Then it builds and compiles
the model inside strategy.scope(),

151
00:08:38,965 --> 00:08:44,055
so every weight and optimizer variable
is mirrored, one copy per GPU.

152
00:08:44,300 --> 00:08:45,900
Here is one training step.

153
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:50,679
Each GPU takes its own slice of the batch
and computes a gradient.

154
00:08:50,999 --> 00:08:54,079
The gradients meet in an all-reduce
and are averaged,

155
00:08:54,079 --> 00:08:56,695
and the same update goes back
to both copies.

156
00:08:56,933 --> 00:08:58,012
Now two machines,

157
00:08:58,012 --> 00:09:02,266
each running its own MirroredStrategy
on its own shard of the data.

158
00:09:02,586 --> 00:09:07,408
Inside each machine the ring works,
but between the machines there is no wire.

159
00:09:07,728 --> 00:09:10,410
After a few steps they
hold different weights:

160
00:09:10,410 --> 00:09:12,101
two separate models, not one.

161
00:09:12,350 --> 00:09:17,001
To train one model across machines,
use MultiWorkerMirroredStrategy.

162
00:09:17,321 --> 00:09:19,428
Each machine reads a TF_CONFIG:

163
00:09:19,428 --> 00:09:23,507
the list of all the workers,
and its own index in that list.

164
00:09:23,827 --> 00:09:25,384
Same shards as before;

165
00:09:25,384 --> 00:09:29,843
the only change is the wire,
and now every copy stays the same.

166
00:09:31,450 --> 00:09:33,204
The repo does not use it;

167
00:09:33,204 --> 00:09:37,765
on Vertex AI the extra machines
would go in a second worker pool,

168
00:09:37,765 --> 00:09:40,431
and Vertex sets the TF_CONFIG for you.

169
00:09:40,751 --> 00:09:43,869
One naming trap: the repo, like the lecture,

170
00:09:43,869 --> 00:09:48,901
calls its replica count num_workers,
but it counts GPUs on one machine.

171
00:09:49,150 --> 00:09:53,101
If the question says
several machines must train one model,

172
00:09:53,101 --> 00:09:56,382
think MultiWorkerMirroredStrategy
with TF_CONFIG.

173
00:09:56,702 --> 00:09:59,902
A MirroredStrategy never looks
past its own machine.

174
00:10:01,150 --> 00:10:02,217
Now the batch.

175
00:10:02,537 --> 00:10:03,731
Under a strategy,

176
00:10:03,731 --> 00:10:07,383
whatever dataset.batch produces
is the global batch,

177
00:10:07,383 --> 00:10:09,321
the whole tray for one step.

178
00:10:09,641 --> 00:10:13,907
The strategy cuts that tray
into equal slices, one per replica.

179
00:10:15,017 --> 00:10:20,500
So the per replica batch is the global batch
divided by the number of replicas in sync.

180
00:10:20,820 --> 00:10:25,875
Turn it around, and the global batch
is the per replica batch times the replicas.

181
00:10:26,117 --> 00:10:29,893
Take 8 replicas,
and say you want 16 examples on each.

182
00:10:30,213 --> 00:10:33,498
Batch by 16, and each GPU gets only 2.

183
00:10:33,818 --> 00:10:35,610
Batch by 128.

184
00:10:35,930 --> 00:10:37,722
Each GPU gets 16.

185
00:10:38,567 --> 00:10:41,127
The repo does the same multiplication.

186
00:10:41,447 --> 00:10:45,504
It takes the batch size flag,
multiplies it by the replica count,

187
00:10:45,504 --> 00:10:48,188
and hands the product to the data pipeline.

188
00:10:48,508 --> 00:10:52,305
Its own comment says it:
batch expects the global batch size.

189
00:10:53,550 --> 00:10:54,787
Second question.

190
00:10:55,107 --> 00:11:01,977
The repo's multi GPU trainer now runs
on 4 GPUs, with a batch size flag of 32.

191
00:11:02,297 --> 00:11:06,030
What is the global batch,
and what does each GPU see per step?

192
00:11:06,350 --> 00:11:07,715
Pause here if you want.

193
00:11:18,550 --> 00:11:19,851
The answer is B.

194
00:11:20,171 --> 00:11:25,186
The trainer multiplies first:
32 times 4 is 128,

195
00:11:25,186 --> 00:11:31,350
and the strategy splits 128 four ways,
so each GPU sees 32.

196
00:11:31,670 --> 00:11:34,273
A: that is a script that never multiplied.

197
00:11:34,593 --> 00:11:38,625
C: the flag is the per replica size,
not the global batch.

198
00:11:38,945 --> 00:11:42,998
D: the global batch is split,
not copied to every GPU.

199
00:11:43,733 --> 00:11:47,584
If the question says per replica,
multiply before you batch:

200
00:11:47,584 --> 00:11:51,499
the global batch is the per replica
batch times the replicas.

201
00:11:52,633 --> 00:11:53,913
Last mechanism.

202
00:11:54,233 --> 00:11:58,222
Suppose a web app has a retrain button,
and training takes an hour.

203
00:11:58,542 --> 00:11:59,972
Where should that hour run?

204
00:12:00,216 --> 00:12:03,779
Every piece of work lives
as long as the thing that holds it.

205
00:12:04,099 --> 00:12:07,875
Inside the request handler,
it dies at the request timeout.

206
00:12:08,116 --> 00:12:12,303
In a server thread, it lives
only as long as that server instance,

207
00:12:12,303 --> 00:12:15,220
which the platform
recycles whenever it likes.

208
00:12:15,466 --> 00:12:18,389
A longer client timeout
moves the wrong clock.

209
00:12:18,709 --> 00:12:21,901
The server and the proxies
still cut the request,

210
00:12:21,901 --> 00:12:24,896
and a sleeping laptop
loses the answer anyway.

211
00:12:25,133 --> 00:12:31,405
And W&B is not a clock at all: it logs runs,
but has no engine to execute them.

212
00:12:31,650 --> 00:12:32,850
The right design:

213
00:12:32,850 --> 00:12:37,154
the handler submits a durable job
and returns its id at once.

214
00:12:37,474 --> 00:12:41,838
The job moves through its states,
queued, pending, running, succeeded,

215
00:12:41,838 --> 00:12:44,706
on its own machine,
billed only while it runs.

216
00:12:45,026 --> 00:12:48,332
Anyone with access
to the project can poll the id.

217
00:12:51,233 --> 00:12:53,824
That is the lecture's serverless training:

218
00:12:53,824 --> 00:12:58,017
hardware rented only for the training step,
and no server to manage.

219
00:12:58,337 --> 00:13:01,686
There is still a machine;
you just do not own its lifetime.

220
00:13:01,933 --> 00:13:06,746
In the repo, the ticket is one command,
gcloud ai custom-jobs create,

221
00:13:06,746 --> 00:13:10,214
with a region,
a name, the package in the bucket,

222
00:13:10,214 --> 00:13:11,489
and a worker pool:

223
00:13:11,489 --> 00:13:15,394
machine type,
replica count, prebuilt image, and module.

224
00:13:16,233 --> 00:13:17,385
Third question.

225
00:13:17,705 --> 00:13:21,673
Your custom job shows running,
and you close the laptop for the night.

226
00:13:21,993 --> 00:13:23,550
What happens to the training?

227
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:25,236
Pause here if you want.

228
00:13:36,083 --> 00:13:37,099
The answer is B:

229
00:13:37,099 --> 00:13:41,096
it keeps running on its own machine
until it finishes or fails.

230
00:13:41,416 --> 00:13:44,360
A: the CLI container only submitted it.

231
00:13:44,680 --> 00:13:48,350
C: W&B only logs; it never drives training.

232
00:13:48,670 --> 00:13:51,720
D: nothing on your laptop
holds the job's state.

233
00:13:52,466 --> 00:13:56,868
If the question says training takes
longer than the request, think:

234
00:13:56,868 --> 00:13:59,890
submit a durable job,
return its id, and poll.

235
00:14:00,210 --> 00:14:02,152
W&B only watches.

236
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:04,900
Six rules for Tuesday.

237
00:14:05,220 --> 00:14:09,994
Rule one: a callback logs one entry
per epoch into the active run;

238
00:14:09,994 --> 00:14:12,815
init opens a run, and finish closes it.

239
00:14:13,050 --> 00:14:16,850
Rule two:
pip installs only what setup.py declares;

240
00:14:16,850 --> 00:14:20,922
your own modules
must be inside the package it builds,

241
00:14:20,922 --> 00:14:23,034
not just inside the tarball.

242
00:14:23,283 --> 00:14:27,863
Rule three: a MirroredStrategy
covers the GPUs of one machine;

243
00:14:27,863 --> 00:14:29,064
across machines,

244
00:14:29,064 --> 00:14:32,591
use MultiWorkerMirroredStrategy
with TF_CONFIG,

245
00:14:32,591 --> 00:14:36,211
because separate strategies
make separate models.

246
00:14:36,450 --> 00:14:38,482
Rule four: under a strategy,

247
00:14:38,482 --> 00:14:41,748
what dataset.batch makes
is the global batch,

248
00:14:41,748 --> 00:14:44,215
split across the replicas in sync.

249
00:14:44,450 --> 00:14:48,718
Rule five: long training goes
to a durable job with an id,

250
00:14:48,718 --> 00:14:53,133
never into a request handler,
a thread, or a longer timeout.

251
00:14:53,367 --> 00:14:55,034
Rule six: a data version

252
00:14:55,034 --> 00:14:59,063
is an archived snapshot plus the record
that points at it.

253
00:14:59,300 --> 00:15:03,070
The lecture began with three levers
for better deep learning:

254
00:15:03,070 --> 00:15:05,913
more data, better models,
and faster hardware.

255
00:15:06,233 --> 00:15:07,862
Today was the hardware lever,

256
00:15:07,862 --> 00:15:10,671
plus the bookkeeping that shows
whether it helped.

257
00:15:10,991 --> 00:15:12,377
Good luck on Tuesday.

